
<p>One more nugget from last night’s 7-0 Yankees win over the Giants:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>During the sixth inning of Wednesday’s <a href="https://www.mlb.com/news/max-fried-yankees-shut-out-giants-on-opening-night-2026">Opening Night matchup
between two historic franchises</a>, the Giants and Yankees,
all-time home run leader <a href="https://www.mlb.com/player/111188">Barry Bonds</a> joined the Netflix
broadcast booth at Oracle Park and told an incredible story about
just how close he came to signing with the Yankees in 1992. [...]</p>

<p>“Well, I would’ve been a Yankees [player],” Bonds said, “but
Steinbrenner got on the phone and they called us and they told me,
‘Barry, we’re gonna give you the money — [make you] the
highest-paid player … but you have to sign the contract by 2:00
this afternoon.’”</p>

<p>One thing you don’t do is give Bonds an ultimatum.</p>

<p>“And I said, ‘Excuse me?’” Bonds said. “And I just hung the
phone up.”</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The Yankees went on to play in six World Series from that moment until the end of Bonds’s playing career, winning four championships. <a href="https://www.statmuse.com/mlb/ask/barry-bonds-career-world-series-stats">Bonds played in one World Series</a> with the Giants, losing a seven-game series to the Angels in 2002.</p>

